y have anywhere else to go unless  we get new evidence and there's a lot of  disagreement over all the police's  police is handling it correctly or not  but as an actual person doing the  investigation is it pretty clear to you  when you've gone as far as you can go  with the evidence you have yeah you  start to you start this sense that that  you're kind of running out of options  you're running out of leads you're  running out of information that will  propel you forward and it's it's  frustrating but there is a perception  that because these cases aren't being  solved that the police aren't doing  anything and you know there's just  there's conventional investigative  approaches there's things that you know  all homicide detectives will have a  process and have essentially checkboxes  you interview people you look at your  physical evidence you you know you  utilize whatever resources are available  to you and and see what it gets you but  sometimes it just doesn't get you  anywhere so when you started I mean when  you actually started digging into this  case what allowed you to go beyond where  it had been left about a decade prior  well I had somewhat of an advantage  because I had come from working in a  kind of a broader federal context and  working with much better resources than  even the LAPD had at Robbery Homicide  Division and they're well-equipped there  but they don't have all of the funding  and the resources that they need to  really undergo the investigative  strategy that we developed for this case  we needed a lot of money and we needed a  lot of reach to be able to travel and to  buy narcotics and to to do the things  that aren't generally done in in a  standard and homicide investigation so  you know we kind of just took it to a  new level  and brought in a lot of resources that  don't exist in a in a entity like  Robbery Homicide Division so ultimately  who do you believe killed Tupac Shakur  Tupac Shakur was killed by a guy named  Orlando Anderson  he was a South Side [ __ ] from Compton he  was the same guy that she fought  Chikara gotten into a fight with earlier  that night at the MGM in the lobby that  individual along with his crew of gang  members all searched out found Tupac and  shot both him and show and ultimately  what's the evidentiary basis for that in  other words I've seen you say that now  we have factual proof of that what is  that proof that proof comes in the form  of eyewitness testimony not just  eyewitness testimony but comes in the  form of co-conspirator testimony the  individual who shot and killed Tupac  Shakur was in a car along with his uncle  his uncle's name was Keith Iggy Dwayne  Davis also a South Side [ __ ] gang member  and Dwayne Davis was compelled to  cooperate during what we call a proper  session at the US Attorney's Office and  he admitted to his involvement he  admitted to handing the gun to his  nephew who was in the back seat he shot  and killed Orlando so we have an  individual a co-conspirator confessing  to the crime and explaining details that  you wouldn't know about the crime unless  you were actually there so it was a very  validated confession under very  compelling circumstances and it's the  best evidence that there's ever been in  a case where is Orlando Anderson now  Orlando Anderson was killed in 1998  outside of a gas station in Compton on  an unrelated gang incident he was trying  to collect money drug a drug debt from a  couple of guys shooting took place and  Orlando Anderson ended up getting shot  killed you mentioned that these two  investigations that into notorious b.i.g  and Tupac Shakur that these  investigations are linked can you talk  about that a little bit what is the  connection well the connection is that  Biggie Smalls was killed in retaliation  for the shooting of Tupac Shakur in Las  Vegas there was an association between  Bad Boy Records and these South Side  Crips the same ones who killed Tupac  Shakur in Las Vegas there was a