 takes place between Tupac and us  [ __ ] gang member and zip who has the gun  says listen let's kill two birds with  one stone they just jumped your nephew  and beat him down plus puffy wants this  stuff taken care of so let's go handle  it let me talk a little more broadly  since we have you here there are now  pretty clear opposing views when it  comes to how people should sort of carry  on if police or someone like you comes  and wants to talk to them and there are  those who say listen whether you might  be involved or not and whatever the  police want to talk to you about there's  really no benefit to talking because you  might accidentally incriminate yourself  you might end up putting yourself in  sort of a worse position you're always  better off not talking to police unless  you're actually arrested and detained  and sort of even in that case you always  want to have a lawyer present  the counterpoint often is listen if  you're really not involved by talking to  the police you are going to probably  take away attention from you that the  act of not talking in and of itself  might make someone like you a homicide  investigator more focused on a potential  witness or suspect what's the truth  about that in your eyes well it's  actually both of those are true you know  you have to evaluate what is the  person's motive not to talk especially  when you would assume they would be  interested in needing justice in the  case such as the cases with Tupac and  Biggie you would think that their  friends and their entourage would be  doing everything they could in order to  try to bring the killers to justice but  unfortunately in these kind of  subcultures whether it's gangs or you  know street culture they have a code not  to cooperate with the police and they  take on the mentality of like it's it's  not my place just solve this homicide  it's not my place to throw somebody's  name out there and put them on the radar  of law enforcement it's you know let the  cops 